lso has symbol \"s1\". Could we remove n4? The instruction says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". If we interpret as any node whose symbol is required cannot be removed, then n4 cannot be removed despite being removable. But is that consistent? The fixture payloads have nodes with required symbol and removable=true (n4). Why would they mark it removable if it cannot be removed? Possibly they intend that we may remove it as long as another node with that symbol remains. So I think \"a required symbol\" means a node that is marked as required (i.e., its id is in some list), but they don't have such field. Alternatively, maybe they mean \"a node that is required for the grammar\" i.e., a non-terminal that is mandatory; but that is exactly the required_symbols list. To resolve, let's look at the sample output? Not given. But we can deduce from typical decision: In Perses, you can remove any node as long as the resulting tree still covers all required non-terminals and is grammar-valid. Since this is a \"miniature grammar\" and \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\", we need to preserve the ability to generate the required symbols. So I will assume that we can remove a node with a required symbol if at least one other node with that symbol remains. However, the instruction explicitly says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". That is contradictory. I'll re-read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" It might be that \"required symbol\" refers to symbols that are required to appear (i.e., the set of symbols must still be present after removal). So it's not node-level but symbol-level. But the phrasing is ambiguous. Given the constraints, I'll go with the interpretation that we cannot remove a node if its symbol is one of the required symbols and there is no other node with that symbol that will remain. But that's more complex. Another interpretation: maybe \"required symbol\" is a node that has \"required\" flag? But there is no such flag. Only \"removable\". I'll check the sample: root has symbol \"root\", which is required. root is not removable. n1 has symbol \"s1\", required, and n1 is not removable. So those are already protected by the removable flag. n4 also has symbol \"s1\" and is removable. If we cannot remove any node with a required symbol, then n4 cannot be removed even though it's removable. But the instruction says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\", and since n4 is a required symbol (s1), we must keep it. That would make all payloads have no removable nodes with required symbols? Let's see: in the first fixture, n2 is removable and symbol \"s2\", which is not required, so removable. n4 is removable and symbol \"s1\" (required). n6 is removable and symbol \"s0\" (not required). So there is at least one removable node with a required symbol. If we keep it, then it's not removed. The algorithm would then only remove n2 and n6? n2 has children n4, so if n4 is retained, n2 cannot be removed because it has retained children. So in that case, only n6 could be removed. But is that the expected result? Perhaps there is a more nuanced rule: we can remove a node with a required symbol if there is another node with the same symbol that will remain. That would allow removal of n4 because n1 also has symbol \"s1\" and is not removable. So n4 could be removed. But then n2 becomes leaf? n2 has child n4, if n4 is removed, n2 becomes leaf (its only child removed). Then n2 can be considered for removal if it's removable and not required. That would lead to removal of both n4 and n2. That seems plausible. There's also n6. So maybe the algorithm removes n4, n2, n6, leaving root, n1, n3, n5. That seems like a reduction that preserves required symbols (root, s1 from n1) and removes redundant nodes.\n\nGiven the description \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\" Interestingness is not defined, but \"required_symbols\" might be that oracle. So we must keep at least one node for each required symbol.\n\nThus I'll adopt: we can remove a removable node if:\n- it is not the root,\n- it has no retained children (i.e., all children were already removed or never had children),\n- its symbol is not required, OR if it is required, there is at least one other retained node with that symbol somewhere else in the tree (but careful: if we remove it, the symbol may still be present elsewhere). However, the instruction \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" might be too strict. I'll check the ordering: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That suggests we process leaves first. So we can compute the set of all nodes, then simulate removal in order of depth descending, and for each node, if removable and not root and (symbol not required or (if required, there is another node with that symbol that is not being removed and is not in its subtree? Actually if the symbol is required and the node is the only one with that symbol, we cannot remove it. But if there is another retained node with that symbol outside its subtree, we can remove it even if symbol required. But the instruction says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\", so it would forbid that. I'll look for consistency: In the fixture, n1 is not removable, so it's always retained. n4 is removable and has same symbol s1. If we cannot remove any node with a required symbol, then n4 is never removed. Then n2 has a child n4 retained, so n2 cannot be removed either. Then only n6 is removable. But n6 has parent n1, which is retained. So n6 can be removed because it has no children and symbol s0 not required. So removed = [n6], retained = all others. That seems too trivial. The problem likely expects more removal. Given the algorithm description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That implies we check the representation, not the node's symbol individually. So we can remove a node with required symbol if the symbol still appears elsewhere. So I'll go with that.\n\nThus algorithm:\n- Build a dict of nodes by id.\n- Build parent-child relationships.\n- Build a set of required symbols.\n- Initialize retained set to all node ids.\n- Create a processing list of node ids sorted by depth descending, then by id (string).\n- Also maintain for each node a set of symbols in its subtree (including itself) that are currently retained? Alternatively, we can compute symbol counts globally and update as we remove.\n- We'll iterate over nodes in that order (deepest first). For each node, if its id is still in retained set, and it is removable (from original data), and it is not the root, and it has no children that are still in retained set (i.e., all its children have been removed or never existed), and after removal, the set of symbols of all retained nodes (including this node's symbol?) still contains all required symbols, then we tentatively remove it. We remove by removing it from retained set, and also update symbol counts? But we need to check if required symbols are still represented. We can maintain a count of each symbol among retained nodes. When we consider removal of a node, we temporarily decrement its symbol count. If any required symbol becomes zero, don't remove; else, finalize removal and decrement count permanently. Also note: root cannot be removed. Also, we must not remove a node that has children still retained; but because we process deeper first, children are processed before parent, so when we get to parent, children may have been removed. So we just check if any child id is still in retained set.\n\nEdge case: a node with no children (leaf) is always candidate if removable.\n\nAfter processing all nodes in order, we have retained set. Then we sort retained_node_ids alphabetically? The output requires removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So removed_node_ids should be in the order they were removed (i.e., the order we processed and removed them). retained_node_ids should be sorted alphabetically? The schema doesn't specify order, but it says \"sorted retained IDs\". So we sort retained_node_ids. For removed, we need the removal order (the order we removed). Since we process nodes in a fixed order (depth descending, then ID), and we only remove when conditions met, that order is the removal order. We should record each removal as we go.\n\nNow, regarding tie break: \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" That means when multiple nodes have same depth, we should process them in order of node ID (assume string compare). In our sorting, we'll sort by (-depth, id). That ensures deeper first, then alphabetical for equal depth.\n\nNow, we must also consider that the algorithm might need to iterate multiple passes? Because after removing a leaf, its parent may become a leaf and can be considered later. Since we process in depth order, we already handle that because parent has lower depth, so it will be processed after all its children (which are deeper) have been processed (and potentially removed). So a single pass from deepest to shallowest should suffice.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Parse case: get nodes list and required_symbols.\n2. Build dict node_by_id: each node dict.\n3. Build adj list children: list of child ids for each parent.\n4. Identify root: node with parent null.\n5. Sort all node ids by depth descending, then by id string.\n6. Initialize retained = set of all ids.\n7. Initialize symbol_counts: dict mapping symbol -> count among all nodes (initially all nodes).\n8. Initialize removed_order = [].\n9. Iterate over sorted ids:\n   - If id not in retained, skip (already removed maybe because parent removed? actually we process all ids, but if a node is removed earlier, it's no longer in retained, but we might still encounter it? No, we only remove nodes that are in retained, and we remove them, so later when we encounter same id, it's not in retained. But we process each id once. So we can skip if not in retained.\n   - Get node from node_by_id.\n   - if not node['removable']: continue.\n   - if id == root id: continue (root not removable).\n   - Check if any child in retained: if yes, continue.\n   - Check symbol requirement: symbol = node['symbol']; if symbol in required_symbols and symbol_counts[symbol] == 1: cannot remove because this is the only node with that symbol. (Note: symbol_counts[symbol] includes this node. So if count == 1, removal would make it zero). So if count == 1 and symbol in required_symbols, skip.\n   - If passes, then remove: remove from retained, decrement symbol_counts[symbol], append id to removed_order.\n10. After loop, retained_node_ids = sorted(list(retained)).\n11. removed_node_ids = removed_order (list in order of removal).\n12. Return dict with keys \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\".\n\nNow, is there any need to consider \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\"? Not needed as we don't have a grammar. The problem says \"miniature grammar\" but parser generation excluded. So we just rely on required_symbols.\n\nAlso, note that \"utility\" is present but not used. It might be for interestingness oracle? But not specified.\n\nWe'll implement accordingly.\n\nNow, we need to output as JSON with \"implementation\" key containing Python source code. The function must be named solve(case). Must not mutate case. Use standard library only. No randomness.\n\nLet's write the code. Ensure it's robust for edge cases (e.g., node id strings). We'll assume node ids are unique. We'll sort by id string.\n\nWe must be careful with the ordering: the description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That is exactly what we do.\n\nOne more nuance: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" We already check symbol count after removal. But what about the root? root is always retained and its symbol is \"root\" which is required. So it's safe.\n\nAlso, we must not remove nodes with retained children. Our check ensures that.\n\nNow, implement.\n\nPotential issue: When we remove a node, we don't remove its ancestors from the retained set, but that's fine. The parent will later be considered if all its children are removed. Our algorithm handles that because when we get to parent, we check if any child is still retained. If all children removed, it passes.\n\nNow, let's write the Python code.\n\nWe'll include comments for clarity but not necessary.\n\nFinally, wrap in JSON with key \"implementation\".\n\nWe need to escape newlines and quotes properly.\n\nLet's craft the solution."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2054,"completion_tokens":5341,"total_tokens":7395,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4909},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":6},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}